/** @page tokenizer_decodeguide Decoding

    @tableofcontents

    @code{.py}
# Decode with special tokens visible
string with_special = tok.decode(enc.input_ids);
# "[CLS] hello , world ! [SEP]"

# Decode without special tokens
string clean = tok.decode(enc.input_ids, True);
# "hello , world !"
    @endcode

    A \c Sequence passes the token list from each decoder to the next. In the TinyLlama tokenizer,
    \c Replace changes each SentencePiece word marker to a space, \c ByteFallback decodes consecutive byte
    tokens as UTF-8, \c Fuse joins the pieces, and \c Strip removes the initial space. Both
    @ref Qore::Tokenizer::HFTokenizer::decode() "decode()" and
    @ref Qore::Tokenizer::HFTokenizer::decodeBatch() "decodeBatch()" apply the configured sequence.

    @code{.py}
# TinyLlama token IDs for a word, a space, and four bytes encoding a fox emoji
string text = tok.decode((1, 904, 6468, 29871, 243, 162, 169, 141), True);
# "élan 🦊"
    @endcode

    Decoded text is UTF-8.  IDs that give only a part of a character, as when the IDs of a text are decoded in
    parts, give the replacement character U+FFFD for the part, as in Hugging Face tokenizers (see
    @ref tokenizer_text_encoding):

    @code{.py}
# GPT-2: "😀" is two byte-level tokens
hash<auto> enc = tok.encode("😀");
string part = tok.decode(enc.input_ids[0..0]);
# "�" (UTF-8)
string whole = tok.decode(enc.input_ids);
# "😀"
    @endcode
*/
